1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,539
Ciao. This is Liam, in for Bear. When an agent loses the thread, the instinct is to

2
00:00:04,559 --> 00:00:09,366
give it more. So don't ask how to give your agent more context. Ask how to give it

3
00:00:09,386 --> 00:00:10,170
the right context.

4
00:00:10,240 --> 00:00:14,286
Three terms. The context window: everything the model sees at once, from

5
00:00:14,306 --> 00:00:19,030
instructions and tools to history and data. Context rot: as that window fills, the

6
00:00:19,050 --> 00:00:23,096
model recalls what's in it less accurately. And context engineering: choosing what

7
00:00:23,116 --> 00:00:25,807
goes in, every time the model is called.

8
00:00:25,877 --> 00:00:30,926
Picture the context window as a tray. Everything the model sees has to fit in it:

9
00:00:30,946 --> 00:00:35,044
the instructions, the tools, the message history, and any data it pulls in.

10
00:00:35,114 --> 00:00:40,264
An agent working in a loop keeps adding to it. Every tool call, every result, lands

11
00:00:40,284 --> 00:00:45,110
in the tray. But more isn't better. As the tray fills, the model recalls what's

12
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:47,695
inside less accurately. Anthropic calls this context rot.

13
00:00:47,765 --> 00:00:52,518
Why? Attention is a budget. Every token attends to every other token, so n tokens

14
00:00:52,538 --> 00:00:56,655
make n squared pairs. Add more, and each one gets a thinner share.

15
00:00:56,725 --> 00:01:01,481
So the goal is the smallest set of high-signal tokens that gets the job done. A

16
00:01:01,501 --> 00:01:05,958
clear prompt. A few tools that don't overlap. A handful of good examples, not a

17
00:01:05,978 --> 00:01:07,450
laundry list of edge cases.

18
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:11,287
Next, don't load everything up front. Keep lightweight references, like file paths

19
00:01:11,307 --> 00:01:16,020
and links, and load the data just in time. Claude Code does this, using commands

20
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:20,122
like head and tail to read big data without loading all of it.

21
00:01:20,192 --> 00:01:24,553
Long tasks outgrow any tray, and Anthropic describes three fixes. First, compaction.

22
00:01:24,573 --> 00:01:29,299
Near the limit, the history gets summarized. In Claude Code, that keeps decisions

23
00:01:29,319 --> 00:01:34,046
and unresolved bugs, and drops redundant tool outputs. Then a fresh window starts

24
00:01:34,066 --> 00:01:35,141
from the summary.

25
00:01:35,211 --> 00:01:39,664
Second, structured note-taking. The agent writes notes to a file outside the window,

26
00:01:39,684 --> 00:01:44,826
like a to-do list. After the context resets, it reads them back and carries on.

27
00:01:44,896 --> 00:01:49,595
Third, sub-agents. The main agent keeps the plan and hands focused jobs to

28
00:01:49,615 --> 00:01:54,315
sub-agents with clean windows. Each may use tens of thousands of tokens exploring,

29
00:01:54,335 --> 00:01:58,671
but returns only a condensed summary, often one to two thousand tokens.

30
00:01:58,741 --> 00:02:03,609
Whichever you use, the rule is the same. Context is a budget. Spend it on the

31
00:02:03,629 --> 00:02:06,970
smallest set of high-signal tokens that gets the next step right.

32
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:11,360
Your turn. Paste this into Claude: Here's a long task I run with an AI agent:

33
00:02:11,380 --> 00:02:15,158
[describe it in two sentences]. List everything that lands in its context. Mark each

34
00:02:15,178 --> 00:02:19,498
item: keep, load only when needed, compact, move to a notes file, or hand to a

35
00:02:19,518 --> 00:02:23,296
sub-agent. Then write the summary you'd keep if the context reset right now. Then

36
00:02:23,316 --> 00:02:27,636
check two things yourself. For each item you kept, does the next step use it? If

37
00:02:27,656 --> 00:02:31,976
not, cut it. And paste only the summary into a fresh chat. Can Claude say what's

38
00:02:31,996 --> 00:02:33,061
done and what's next?

39
00:02:33,131 --> 00:02:37,218
Context Is a Budget. At Nik Bear Brown.
